items.does:
pagecheck.js reads each NEW page of the language's ported sections, per variation, and checks four things:
- STEPS: a step count ('these N steps', number words too, 'this step') against the ordered list that follows it before the next heading;
- HEADING: each numbered heading against its place among the numbered headings under the same parent;
- LIST: each ordered list counts 1, 2, 3 in the order printed;
- ARTICLE: each a/an against the word or code identifier after it. A vowel sound takes 'an'; an acronym is read letter by letter; NULL, NIL, TRUE and FALSE are read as words; one/once and eu/ewe take 'a'; u- and h-words go by lists. A symbol, a digit, an all-capital code name or a u-word outside the lists is counted as not judged.

The one command runs it (portcheck step 'page numbers and articles') and fails on any finding. Nothing can be accepted by hand. Identity and the match form still set numbers and the article aside by design. Keeping them literal in the match form would not help, because matching is across variations, where entity and step count differ.

items.still_fooled_by:
Five gaps remain:
- an article before a code token starting with a symbol (go's 'a `[]any`', 102 not judged);
- a u-word outside the lists (not judged);
- a heading moved to a deeper level and numbered 1 there (my attack: not caught);
- a step removed together with its count, which leaves the page self-consistent (the ledger sees it as a missing unit);
- number words other than step counts ('the two layers') are not checked.

Three cases remain:
- a sentence printed by all references but one is outside the DROPPED set;
- a style-bound sentence only one reference prints, such as go's tutorial step bodies (recheck go-tut-step4-dropped [scratch] stays moved);
- a language that drops a sentence in a variation where it also has a constant shortfall elsewhere, which hides a drop of the same size.

